# pairing_two_controllers.scn — two simultaneous controllers during Pairing;
# the vehicle must bind to the one that addresses IT, never to the one that
# addresses a different vehicle (own-address acceptance, pairing-methods-plan.md
# §4.1/§4.4 — the fix for cross-pairing when many pairs are formed at once).
#
# RUN WITH --unpaired so the vehicle boots UNPAIRED (skips the SILS pairing NVS seed):
#   sf sils scenario simulator/sils/scenarios/pairing_two_controllers.scn --target vehicle --unpaired
#
#   1. Unpaired boot -> StateManager auto-enters Pairing (comm broadcasts a PairingPacket).
#   2. Phase A (Controller A ONLY): a ControlPacket addressed to a DIFFERENT vehicle
#      (drone_mac != this vehicle's own MAC) arrives repeatedly from Controller A's
#      MAC. The own-address filter must REJECT every one of them as a pending-bind
#      candidate -> the vehicle stays in Pairing (never binds to A) for this whole
#      phase, even though A is the only transmitter on the air.
#   3. Phase B (Controller B): a DIFFERENT transmitter (Controller B's MAC) now sends
#      a ControlPacket correctly addressed to THIS vehicle -> comm accepts it as the
#      pending-bind candidate on its first packet -> Paired.
#
#  <t>  ch          <thr> <roll> <pitch> <yaw> <arm> <hold_ms> <rate_hz>
   0    rc_ctrl_a   2048  2048   2048    2048  0     5000      50   # A: WRONG-address Tx only, disarmed 5 s — own-address filter must reject every packet (boot + auto-Pairing settle)
   +    rc_ctrl_b   2048  2048   2048    2048  0     3000      50   # B: correctly-addressed Tx starts — binds on its first packet, disarmed 3 s hold
